/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · The human body and brain */
WB.L("heart-blood", "body", "beginner", "Your heart and blood",
  "Your heart beats about 100,000 times a day, pushing blood through a network of vessels that, by common estimates, could wrap around the Earth more than twice.",
  [
    ["A tireless pump", "The heart is a muscular pump about the size of a fist that beats around 100,000 times a day.",
      "Your heart sits in the middle of your chest, tilted slightly left, and is roughly the size of your clenched fist. It's made of cardiac muscle, which is built to resist fatigue in a way skeletal muscle isn't. At rest it beats about 60–100 times a minute, around 100,000 times a day and roughly 2.5 to 3 billion times in a lifetime. Each beat is triggered by an electrical signal from a natural pacemaker, the sinoatrial node, in the upper right chamber. That signal spreads through the heart in a set sequence, so the upper chambers squeeze first and the lower chambers follow a fraction of a second later."],
    ["Four chambers, two circuits", "The right side pumps blood to the lungs; the left side pumps it to the rest of the body.",
      "The heart has four chambers: two upper atria that receive blood and two lower ventricles that pump it out. The right side collects oxygen-poor blood from the body and sends it on a short trip to the lungs to pick up oxygen and drop off carbon dioxide. The left side receives oxygen-rich blood from the lungs and pumps it to the whole body, from your brain to your toes. The left ventricle has the thickest wall because it pushes hardest. Four one-way valves keep blood flowing in the right direction, and their closing makes the familiar 'lub-dub' sound a doctor hears through a stethoscope."],
    ["Blood vessels", "Arteries carry blood away from the heart, veins carry it back, and capillaries connect them.",
      "Arteries have thick, elastic walls to handle high pressure from each heartbeat; the pulse you feel in your wrist or neck is an artery stretching. The biggest, the aorta, is roughly as wide as a garden hose. Arteries branch into tiny capillaries, so narrow that red blood cells pass in single file, where oxygen and nutrients move into tissues and waste moves out. Veins carry blood back to the heart under much lower pressure, relying on one-way valves and squeezing leg muscles to push it along. Laid end to end, an adult's blood vessels would stretch for tens of thousands of kilometres, most of it capillaries."],
    ["What blood is made of", "Blood contains red cells, white cells, platelets and plasma.",
      "An adult has about five litres of blood. Just over half is plasma, a yellowish liquid carrying nutrients, hormones, salts and proteins. Red blood cells, made in bone marrow, carry oxygen using haemoglobin, an iron-rich protein that makes blood red. They're shaped like flattened discs, which gives them more surface area and lets them squeeze through tight capillaries. White blood cells, far fewer in number, fight infection. Platelets are cell fragments that clump together to plug wounds and start clotting. Red blood cells live about 120 days, and your body replaces them at a rate of roughly two million every second."],
    ["Blood types and donation", "Blood groups depend on markers on red cells, and matching them makes transfusions safe.",
      "Red blood cells carry markers called antigens. The ABO system sorts blood into groups A, B, AB and O, and the Rh factor adds positive or negative. Giving someone blood with markers their immune system doesn't recognise can trigger a dangerous reaction, which is why blood is carefully matched. O negative can be given to almost anyone in an emergency, so it's in constant demand. AB positive people can usually receive red cells from any group. In Australia, donations are collected by Australian Red Cross Lifeblood. One donation is split into parts, such as red cells, plasma and platelets, so it can help several patients."],
    ["Blood pressure", "Blood pressure measures the force of blood on artery walls; high pressure damages the heart and vessels.",
      "Blood pressure is written as two numbers in millimetres of mercury, like 120/80. The top number, systolic, is the pressure when the heart beats; the bottom, diastolic, is the pressure between beats. It naturally rises and falls through the day with activity, stress and sleep. Persistently high blood pressure, hypertension, often has no symptoms but strains the heart and damages arteries, raising the risk of heart attack, stroke and kidney disease. That's why it's called a silent killer, and why regular checks matter, especially from middle age. Checks are quick, painless and available from GPs and many pharmacies."],
    ["The heart's own supply", "Coronary arteries feed the heart muscle; a blockage in one causes a heart attack.",
      "The heart pumps blood to every organ, but it can't feed itself from the blood inside its chambers. Instead, its muscle is supplied by coronary arteries that wrap around its surface like a crown, which is where the name comes from. Over decades, fatty plaque can build up inside these arteries, a process called atherosclerosis, narrowing them. If a plaque ruptures, a clot can form and block the artery. The heart muscle beyond it is starved of oxygen and starts to die: that's a heart attack. Fast treatment to reopen the artery saves muscle, which is why every minute counts."],
    ["Looking after your heart", "Exercise, not smoking, healthy eating and managing blood pressure protect your heart.",
      "Heart disease is one of the leading causes of death in Australia. The main risk factors are smoking, high blood pressure, high cholesterol, diabetes, physical inactivity, obesity and too much alcohol. Many are modifiable. Regular exercise strengthens the heart; eating more vegetables, whole grains, legumes and fish and less salt helps too. Adults aged 45 and over (30 and over for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people) can ask their GP for a Heart Health Check. Know the signs of a heart attack, like chest pain or pressure, pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back, and breathlessness, and call 000 immediately."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "About how many times does the heart beat each day?", ["1,000", "10,000", "100,000", "1,000,000"], 2, "Around 60–100 times a minute at rest."],
    [1, "Where does the right side of the heart send blood?", ["To the brain", "To the legs", "To the stomach", "To the lungs"], 3, "To pick up oxygen."],
    [2, "What do capillaries do?", ["Connect arteries and veins, exchanging oxygen and waste with tissues", "Pump blood", "Store blood", "Make blood cells"], 0, "Red cells pass in single file."],
    [3, "What makes blood red?", ["Plasma", "Haemoglobin, an iron-rich protein", "White cells", "Platelets"], 1, "It carries oxygen."],
    [5, "Why is high blood pressure called a silent killer?", ["It's quiet", "It only happens at night", "It often has no symptoms but causes serious damage", "It's rare"], 2, "Regular checks matter."],
  ]);

WB.L("digestion", "body", "beginner", "How digestion works",
  "From first bite to final exit, your food travels through several metres of tubing and takes a day or more. Here's what happens along the way.",
  [
    ["The journey begins", "Digestion starts in the mouth, where teeth and saliva begin breaking down food.",
      "The digestive tract is a long tube running from mouth to anus, often quoted as around nine metres in an adult, though it's shorter in a living body with its muscles toned. Chewing breaks food into smaller pieces, and saliva contains an enzyme, amylase, that starts breaking starch into sugar. That's why bread tastes slightly sweet if you chew it for a long time. You make roughly half a litre to one and a half litres of saliva a day. Swallowing pushes food into the oesophagus, where waves of muscle contraction, called peristalsis, move it down to the stomach, even if you're upside down."],
    ["The stomach", "The stomach churns food with acid and enzymes into a thick liquid called chyme.",
      "The stomach is a stretchy muscular bag that can hold about a litre or more after a big meal. Its muscular walls churn food and mix it with strong hydrochloric acid, which kills many microbes, and the enzyme pepsin, which starts digesting protein. A layer of mucus protects the stomach lining from its own acid, and the lining itself is renewed every few days. Some things are absorbed here, including alcohol. After a few hours, food becomes a thick liquid called chyme, and a ring of muscle, the pyloric sphincter, releases it gradually into the small intestine."],
    ["The small intestine", "Most digestion and nutrient absorption happens in the small intestine.",
      "Despite its name, the small intestine is the longest part of the tract, several metres long and only a few centimetres wide. Here, bile from the liver breaks fat into small droplets, and enzymes from the pancreas break down carbohydrates, proteins and fats into small molecules like sugars, amino acids and fatty acids. The intestine's wall is covered in millions of tiny finger-like villi, themselves covered in even smaller microvilli, which give it a huge surface area to absorb nutrients into the blood. Food typically spends several hours here, and most of digestion's real work happens along this stretch."],
    ["Liver and pancreas", "The liver processes nutrients and makes bile; the pancreas makes enzymes and insulin.",
      "Blood from the intestines goes first to the liver, which processes nutrients, stores energy as glycogen, and removes toxins, including alcohol. It's the largest internal organ and can regenerate remarkably after damage. It also makes bile, which is stored and concentrated in the gall bladder. The pancreas, tucked behind the stomach, produces digestive enzymes and hormones, including insulin, which helps cells absorb sugar from the blood. Diabetes involves problems with insulin: in type 1, the body makes little or none because the cells that produce it are destroyed; in type 2, cells stop responding to it well."],
    ["The large intestine", "The large intestine absorbs water and hosts trillions of gut bacteria.",
      "What's left, mostly fibre, water and bacteria, passes into the large intestine, or colon, about 1.5 metres long. Here water and salts are absorbed, turning liquid waste into solid stool, which can take a day or more. The colon is home to trillions of bacteria, the gut microbiome, which ferment fibre, produce some vitamins such as vitamin K, and help train the immune system. When they ferment fibre, they make short-chain fatty acids that help nourish the colon lining. Researchers are actively studying links between the microbiome and health, though many popular claims go beyond current evidence."],
    ["Hunger and fullness", "Hormones and nerves between gut and brain tell you when to eat and when to stop.",
      "Your gut and brain talk constantly, through hormones and through the vagus nerve. When your stomach is empty, it releases ghrelin, a hormone that increases hunger. As you eat, the stomach stretches and the intestine releases hormones that signal fullness. Fat tissue releases leptin, which helps regulate appetite over the longer term. These signals take time to register, which is one reason eating quickly makes it easier to overeat. Sleep, stress and habits also influence appetite: short sleep, for example, is linked to stronger hunger. The gut even has its own vast network of nerves, sometimes called the second brain."],
    ["When things go wrong", "Reflux, food intolerances, coeliac disease and irritable bowel are common gut problems.",
      "Reflux happens when stomach acid flows back into the oesophagus, causing heartburn; large meals, lying down after eating and alcohol can make it worse. Many adults produce less lactase, the enzyme that digests milk sugar, after childhood, so dairy may cause bloating. Coeliac disease is an immune reaction to gluten that damages the small intestine; it affects roughly 1 in 70 Australians, and most don't know they have it. Irritable bowel syndrome causes pain and changed bowel habits without visible damage. Because symptoms overlap, it's worth seeing a GP for a proper diagnosis rather than cutting out foods on your own."],
    ["Keeping it healthy", "Fibre, water, plants and movement support healthy digestion.",
      "Most Australians eat less fibre than recommended. Fibre from vegetables, fruit, legumes and whole grains keeps bowel movements regular and feeds healthy gut bacteria. Eating a wide variety of plant foods may support a more diverse microbiome. Drinking water, staying active and not rushing meals help too. Probiotics may help in some specific situations, but they're not a cure-all. See a doctor about persistent changes in bowel habits, blood in stool, or unexplained weight loss. Australia's National Bowel Cancer Screening Program offers free home test kits from age 45, and they're mailed out automatically every two years from 50 to 74."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is peristalsis?", ["Chewing", "Stomach acid", "A type of enzyme", "Waves of muscle contraction that move food along"], 3, "It works even upside down."],
    [1, "What protects the stomach from its own acid?", ["Bile", "A layer of mucus", "Bacteria", "Water"], 1, "Hydrochloric acid is strong."],
    [2, "Where does most nutrient absorption happen?", ["The small intestine", "The stomach", "The large intestine", "The mouth"], 0, "Villi give it a huge surface area."],
    [3, "Which organ makes insulin?", ["The liver", "The stomach", "The pancreas", "The kidneys"], 2, "It helps cells absorb sugar."],
    [4, "What lives in the large intestine?", ["Nothing", "Trillions of bacteria, the gut microbiome", "Red blood cells", "Enzymes only"], 1, "They ferment fibre."],
  ]);

WB.L("muscles-bones", "body", "beginner", "Muscles and bones",
  "Your skeleton is living tissue that rebuilds itself constantly, and your muscles are the engines that move it.",
  [
    ["The skeleton", "An adult skeleton has 206 bones, supporting and protecting the body.",
      "Babies are born with around 270 to 300 bones or bony pieces, many of which fuse as they grow, leaving about 206 in adults. The soft spots on a baby's skull, called fontanelles, are gaps that close as the skull bones join. The skeleton supports your body, protects organs (the skull guards the brain; the ribs protect the heart and lungs), and anchors muscles. More than half your bones are in your hands and feet. The largest bone is the femur, in the thigh; the smallest is the stapes, in the middle ear, about the size of a grain of rice."],
    ["Living bone", "Bones are living tissue that constantly break down and rebuild.",
      "Bone isn't dead and dry. It's living tissue with blood vessels and nerves, made of a flexible protein framework, mostly collagen, hardened with calcium and phosphate minerals. That mix makes it both strong and slightly springy. Cells called osteoclasts break down old bone, and osteoblasts build new bone, so your skeleton is gradually renewed over years. Inside many bones is marrow, which produces blood cells. Bones store calcium and release it when the body needs it. They also respond to stress: weight-bearing exercise makes bones denser and stronger, while long bed rest or spaceflight makes them lose mass."],
    ["Joints", "Joints connect bones, with cartilage and fluid allowing smooth movement.",
      "Where bones meet, joints allow movement. Hinge joints, like the knee and elbow, bend mainly one way; ball-and-socket joints, like the hip and shoulder, rotate in many directions. The shoulder is the most mobile joint in the body, which is also why it dislocates more easily. Smooth cartilage covers bone ends, and synovial fluid lubricates them. Cartilage has no blood supply, so it heals poorly, and its wear is part of osteoarthritis. Ligaments connect bone to bone, and tendons connect muscle to bone. The Achilles tendon, at the back of the ankle, is the body's strongest."],
    ["How muscles work", "Muscles can only pull, not push, so they work in opposing pairs.",
      "The body has over 600 skeletal muscles. Muscle fibres contain protein filaments, actin and myosin, that slide past each other when a nerve signal arrives, making the muscle shorten and pull on a bone. Muscles can only pull, so they work in pairs: to bend your elbow, the biceps contracts while the triceps relaxes; to straighten it, the reverse. There are also smooth muscles, in organs like the gut and blood vessels, which work without conscious control, and cardiac muscle in the heart. Working muscles also produce a lot of heat, which is why shivering warms you up."],
    ["Fast and slow fibres", "Slow-twitch fibres suit endurance, while fast-twitch fibres deliver speed and power.",
      "Skeletal muscles contain a mix of fibre types. Slow-twitch fibres contract relatively gently but resist fatigue, using oxygen efficiently; they power posture, walking and long-distance running. Fast-twitch fibres contract quickly and powerfully but tire sooner; they're recruited for sprinting, jumping and heavy lifting. The mix varies between muscles and between people, partly because of genes, which helps explain why some people are naturally drawn to endurance or power sports. Training can't completely change your fibre mix, but it can make both types work much better. Endurance training improves stamina, while strength training makes fibres thicker and stronger."],
    ["How bones heal", "Broken bones heal in stages, first with a soft callus and then new hard bone.",
      "When a bone breaks, bleeding forms a clot around the break. Within days, cells lay down a soft callus of cartilage and fibrous tissue that bridges the gap. Over the following weeks, osteoblasts replace it with a harder bony callus, and over months the bone is remodelled back towards its original shape. Many simple fractures in adults take roughly six to eight weeks to heal enough for normal use, though full remodelling takes longer, and children heal faster. Casts and splints hold the pieces in line so they knit correctly. Smoking slows bone healing noticeably."],
    ["Use it or lose it", "Muscle and bone strength decline with age and inactivity but respond to exercise at any age.",
      "From around your 30s or 40s, muscle mass slowly declines, and in older age this loss of muscle and strength is called sarcopenia. Bone density also falls, especially in women after menopause, raising the risk of osteoporosis, where bones become fragile and break easily, often at the hip, spine or wrist. Weak muscles also make falls more likely, and falls are a major cause of injury in older Australians. The good news: strength training and weight-bearing exercise, like walking or stair climbing, build muscle and bone at any age, even in people in their 80s and 90s."],
    ["Protecting your frame", "Calcium, vitamin D, protein and regular exercise keep muscles and bones strong.",
      "Bones need calcium, found in dairy foods, some leafy greens, canned fish with bones and fortified foods, and vitamin D, which your skin makes from sunlight, to absorb it. In Australia, balance sun protection with enough exposure, as advised for your skin type and region. Muscles need enough protein spread across the day, and regular activity. Australian guidelines suggest adults do muscle-strengthening activities on at least two days a week. Avoiding smoking and excess alcohol helps both. Good technique when lifting reduces injury risk. If you're concerned about bone health, a GP can check your risk and arrange a bone density scan."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "How many bones does an adult skeleton have?", ["106", "206", "306", "406"], 1, "Babies have more, which fuse."],
    [1, "What do osteoblasts do?", ["Build new bone", "Break down bone", "Make blood", "Connect muscles"], 0, "Bone is constantly renewed."],
    [2, "What connects muscle to bone?", ["Ligaments", "Cartilage", "Marrow", "Tendons"], 3, "Ligaments connect bone to bone."],
    [3, "Why do muscles work in pairs?", ["For symmetry", "They can only pull, not push", "To save energy", "They're lazy"], 1, "Biceps and triceps, for example."],
    [6, "What builds muscle and bone at any age?", ["Resting", "Supplements alone", "Strength training and weight-bearing exercise", "Sleeping more"], 2, "Even in people in their 90s."],
  ]);

WB.L("senses", "body", "beginner", "How your senses work",
  "You have far more than five senses. And every one of them is your brain's interpretation, not a direct window on the world.",
  [
    ["More than five", "Beyond sight, hearing, smell, taste and touch, we sense balance, body position, temperature, pain and more.",
      "The idea of five senses goes back to Aristotle. Scientists now count many more, depending on how you define a sense. Proprioception tells you where your limbs are without looking, which is how you can touch your nose with your eyes closed. The vestibular system in your inner ear senses balance and motion. Separate receptors detect temperature and pain. Interoception tells you about your body's internal state, like hunger, thirst, a full bladder or a racing heart. All of them work the same basic way: specialised receptor cells convert physical signals into nerve impulses the brain interprets."],
    ["Sight", "The eye focuses light onto the retina, where rods and cones convert it into nerve signals.",
      "Light enters through the cornea and lens, which focus it onto the retina at the back of the eye. The iris adjusts the pupil's size to control how much light gets in. The retina contains about 120 million rods, which work in dim light but don't see colour, and about 6 million cones, which detect colour in three types, roughly sensitive to red, green and blue. Cones are packed into a small central spot, the fovea, which gives your sharpest vision. The image on the retina is upside down; the brain flips it. Each eye also has a blind spot where the optic nerve leaves, which your brain fills in."],
    ["Hearing", "Sound waves vibrate the eardrum and tiny bones; hair cells in the cochlea turn vibrations into signals.",
      "Sound waves travel into the ear canal and vibrate the eardrum. Three tiny bones, the hammer, anvil and stirrup, amplify the vibrations and pass them to the cochlea, a fluid-filled spiral about the size of a pea. Inside, thousands of hair cells bend with the vibrations, with different parts responding to different pitches. Young people can typically hear from about 20 to 20,000 vibrations per second, and the upper limit drops with age. Loud noise can permanently damage these hair cells, which don't regrow in humans. That's why hearing protection matters."],
    ["Balance and motion", "The inner ear's vestibular system senses head movement and gravity to keep you balanced.",
      "Next to the cochlea sit three fluid-filled semicircular canals, arranged at roughly right angles to each other. When you turn your head, the fluid lags behind and bends tiny hair cells, signalling rotation in any direction. Two small organs nearby contain tiny crystals that shift with gravity and straight-line movement, telling you which way is up and when you speed up in a car or lift. The brain combines these signals with vision and proprioception. When they disagree, as when reading in a moving car, the result can be motion sickness. Spinning makes you dizzy because the fluid keeps moving after you stop."],
    ["Smell and taste", "Smell wires almost directly into the brain's emotion and memory centres, and taste and smell cells are constantly replaced.",
      "Taste and smell are chemical senses: they detect molecules rather than light or sound. You have several thousand taste buds, mostly on the tongue, and their receptor cells wear out and are replaced roughly every week or two, which is why a burnt tongue recovers. Smell is the richer sense. Humans have around 400 types of smell receptor, and combinations of them let us tell apart a very large number of odours, far more than once believed. Smell signals travel almost directly to brain areas for emotion and memory, which is why a scent can instantly bring back a place. Smell cells are renewed too, but loss of smell becomes more common with age, and a sudden loss is worth mentioning to a doctor."],
    ["Touch", "The skin has several types of receptors for pressure, vibration, temperature and pain.",
      "Your skin, the body's largest organ, contains different receptors for light touch, pressure, vibration, stretch, heat, cold and pain. They're not evenly spread: fingertips and lips are packed with them, while your back has far fewer. That's why a map of the brain's touch areas, the 'homunculus', has huge hands and lips. Many touch receptors also adapt quickly, which is why you stop noticing your clothes soon after dressing. Pain receptors warn of damage, and people born unable to feel pain, a rare genetic condition, often suffer serious injuries without realising it."],
    ["The brain constructs reality", "Senses provide data, but the brain combines and interprets it to build our experience.",
      "Your brain combines information from different senses, fills gaps, and makes predictions. What you see changes what you hear, as in the McGurk effect, where watching lips say one sound makes you hear a different one. Expectations change how things taste, which is why the same wine can taste better when people are told it's expensive. Optical illusions reveal the shortcuts your visual system takes. Some people experience synaesthesia, where senses blend, such as seeing colours when hearing music. Perception, in short, is an active construction, not a camera recording."],
    ["Looking after your senses", "Protect your eyes and ears, get regular checks and notice changes early.",
      "Many sensory losses are gradual and preventable. Australia's strong sunlight makes good sunglasses and hats worthwhile, as UV exposure contributes to cataracts and other eye problems. Regular eye tests can pick up conditions like glaucoma before you notice any vision loss. For hearing, a common workplace guide is that noise averaging 85 decibels over eight hours is the limit before protection is needed, and louder sounds are safe for much less time; earbuds at high volume count. Sudden loss of smell, hearing or vision, or new dizziness, is worth seeing a doctor about promptly."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is proprioception?", ["Smell", "Hearing", "Sensing where your limbs are without looking", "Balance only"], 2, "One of many senses beyond five."],
    [1, "What do cones in the retina detect?", ["Dim light only", "Colour", "Motion only", "Heat"], 1, "Rods work in dim light."],
    [2, "Why is loud noise so dangerous for hearing?", ["It damages hair cells that don't regrow", "It blocks the ear canal", "It breaks the eardrum instantly", "It causes infections"], 0, "Protect your hearing."],
    [4, "Why can a smell instantly bring back a memory?", ["Smell signals travel almost directly to brain areas for emotion and memory", "The nose stores memories", "Smells are processed by the eyes", "Taste buds record past meals"], 0, "Smell is closely wired to memory and emotion."],
    [5, "Why does the brain's 'homunculus' have huge hands and lips?", ["They're strong", "They're packed with touch receptors", "They move most", "It's a cartoon"], 1, "Touch receptors aren't evenly spread."],
  ]);

WB.L("neurons", "body", "intermediate", "How neurons talk",
  "Every thought, memory and movement comes down to cells sending electrical and chemical messages. Here's how they do it.",
  [
    ["The neuron", "Neurons are cells specialised to send signals, with dendrites, a cell body and an axon.",
      "A neuron has three main parts. Branching dendrites receive signals from other neurons. The cell body, containing the nucleus, combines those inputs. A long, thin axon carries the output signal away, sometimes over a metre, like those running from your lower spine to your toes. Many axons are wrapped in myelin, a fatty insulating layer that speeds signals up enormously; the signal jumps between small gaps in the myelin rather than creeping along. The brain has around 86 billion neurons, a figure from careful cell-counting studies, plus a similar number of supporting glial cells."],
    ["The electrical signal", "Neurons fire an all-or-nothing electrical pulse called an action potential.",
      "A neuron at rest keeps a small voltage across its membrane, about minus 70 millivolts, by pumping charged particles, ions, in and out. When enough input arrives to push it past a threshold, channels open, sodium ions rush in, and a spike of voltage, the action potential, races down the axon. Potassium then flows out to reset it. It's all-or-nothing: a neuron either fires or it doesn't. Stronger stimuli make neurons fire more often, not more strongly. Signals in thin, unmyelinated fibres crawl at about a metre per second, while in myelinated axons they can travel over 100 metres per second."],
    ["The synapse", "At synapses, neurons pass signals using chemical messengers called neurotransmitters.",
      "Most neurons don't quite touch. Between them is a tiny gap, the synapse, far narrower than a human hair. When an action potential reaches the end of an axon, it releases neurotransmitter molecules from little packets called vesicles. These cross the gap in a fraction of a millisecond and bind to receptors on the next neuron, making it more or less likely to fire. Then the neurotransmitters are broken down or recycled back into the sending cell. Each neuron may connect to thousands of others, creating an estimated 100 trillion or more synapses in the human brain."],
    ["Excite and inhibit", "Some signals push neurons to fire, others hold them back; balance is essential.",
      "Glutamate is the brain's main excitatory neurotransmitter, encouraging neurons to fire. GABA is the main inhibitory one, calming activity down. Each neuron is constantly adding up excitatory and inhibitory inputs to decide whether to fire. The brain depends on balancing the two. Too much excitation can cause seizures, which is why many epilepsy drugs boost inhibition or dampen excitation. Alcohol and some sedative medicines enhance GABA's effects, which is part of why they slow reactions and cause drowsiness. Other neurotransmitters, like dopamine and serotonin, act more like volume controls, tuning whole networks."],
    ["Neurons that fire together", "Connections between neurons strengthen with use, the basis of learning.",
      "In 1949, psychologist Donald Hebb proposed that when one neuron repeatedly helps another fire, the connection between them strengthens. This is often summarised as 'neurons that fire together, wire together'. In 1973, researchers reported a mechanism called long-term potentiation in the hippocampus, a memory hub, where repeated stimulation made synapses stronger for hours or longer. Practice physically strengthens pathways, adding receptors and even new connections, while unused connections can weaken and be pruned. This is how skills and memories are built, and why regular practice and review work better than a single long session."],
    ["The other brain cells", "Glial cells insulate, feed, protect and clean up after neurons.",
      "Neurons get the attention, but glial cells are just as numerous and essential. Oligodendrocytes wrap axons in the brain and spinal cord with myelin; Schwann cells do the same in the rest of the body. Astrocytes, star-shaped cells, help supply energy to neurons, regulate chemicals around synapses and help maintain the barrier between blood and brain. Microglia are the brain's immune cells, clearing debris and damaged cells and helping prune unused synapses during development. Once seen as mere glue, which is what 'glia' means in Greek, these cells are now known to take an active part in how the brain works."],
    ["When signalling goes wrong", "Many brain conditions involve problems with neurons, myelin or neurotransmitters.",
      "In multiple sclerosis, the immune system attacks myelin, slowing or blocking signals and causing symptoms like numbness, weakness or vision problems. Parkinson's disease involves the loss of dopamine-producing neurons, affecting movement. Alzheimer's disease damages synapses and neurons, especially in memory areas, and involves build-ups of abnormal proteins. Many medicines work at synapses: some antidepressants, for example, affect how serotonin is recycled, and some anaesthetics and pain relievers act on specific receptors. Understanding how neurons talk remains one of the most important frontiers in medicine."],
    ["Reading neurons' signals", "Brain-computer interfaces decode neurons' electrical activity, letting some paralysed people control computers by thought.",
      "Because neurons communicate electrically, their activity can be recorded and decoded. Brain-computer interfaces place sensors on or near the motor cortex, the strip of brain that plans movement, and use software to translate patterns of firing into commands. In trials in the early 2000s, people with paralysis learned to move a computer cursor simply by imagining moving their hand. An Australian-developed approach, the Stentrode from Melbourne company Synchron, avoids open brain surgery: it's threaded up through the jugular vein into a blood vessel beside the motor cortex. From 2019, its first recipients, people with motor neurone disease, used it to text, shop and bank online. The technology is still experimental, but it's advancing quickly."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What does the axon do?", ["Receives signals", "Stores memories", "Carries the output signal away from the cell body", "Produces blood"], 2, "Dendrites receive signals."],
    [1, "What is special about an action potential?", ["It varies in strength", "It's all-or-nothing", "It travels backwards", "It's chemical only"], 1, "Stronger stimuli mean more frequent firing."],
    [2, "What happens to neurotransmitters after they've passed on their signal?", ["They stay in the gap permanently", "They turn into myelin", "They're broken down or recycled back into the sending cell", "They travel on to the next synapse"], 2, "Clearing them quickly lets the synapse send its next message."],
    [3, "What is GABA?", ["The main excitatory neurotransmitter", "A hormone", "A brain region", "The main inhibitory neurotransmitter"], 3, "It calms activity."],
    [4, "What does 'neurons that fire together, wire together' describe?", ["Seizures", "Connections strengthening with use", "Sleep", "Pain"], 1, "Proposed by Donald Hebb in 1949."],
  ]);

WB.L("brain-chemicals", "body", "intermediate", "Dopamine, serotonin and friends",
  "Pop culture calls dopamine the 'pleasure chemical' and serotonin the 'happy chemical'. The truth is more interesting.",
  [
    ["Chemical messengers", "Neurotransmitters and hormones carry messages that shape mood, motivation and behaviour.",
      "Neurotransmitters carry signals between neurons across tiny gaps; hormones travel through the blood to affect distant organs, working over seconds to days. Some, like adrenaline, act as both. Each chemical affects many systems, and its effects depend on which receptors and brain regions are involved. Dopamine alone has five main receptor types. So simple labels like 'the happy chemical' are misleading. You also can't measure your brain's levels with a blood test. Still, knowing a few key players helps make sense of motivation, mood, stress and how many medicines and drugs work."],
    ["Dopamine: wanting, not liking", "Dopamine drives motivation and learning from rewards more than pleasure itself.",
      "Dopamine is often called the pleasure chemical, but research by Kent Berridge and others suggests it's more about wanting than liking: motivation, anticipation and learning which actions lead to rewards. Dopamine neurons fire strongly when a reward is better than expected, and dip when it's worse, teaching the brain to repeat what worked. Unpredictable rewards, like slot machines or social media notifications, tap into this system powerfully. Dopamine is also essential for movement, which is why its loss in Parkinson's disease affects motor control, and why treatment often involves a drug the brain converts into dopamine."],
    ["Serotonin", "Serotonin influences mood, sleep, appetite and digestion; most of it is in the gut.",
      "Serotonin affects mood, sleep, appetite and impulse control. Surprisingly, around 90% of the body's serotonin is found in the gut, where it helps regulate digestion, and it can't easily cross from the blood into the brain. The popular idea that depression is simply caused by low serotonin is now considered too simple; a widely publicised 2022 review found no consistent evidence for it, though some researchers disputed how it was reported. Antidepressants that affect serotonin help many people, but probably through more complex effects, such as on brain plasticity. Decisions about medication should always be made with a doctor."],
    ["Oxytocin", "Oxytocin supports bonding, trust and childbirth, but it's no simple 'love hormone'.",
      "Oxytocin is made in the hypothalamus and released by the pituitary gland. It triggers contractions during childbirth and milk release during breastfeeding, and it's involved in bonding between parents and babies, and between partners. It's often called the love or cuddle hormone. But research shows its effects depend on context: some studies found it can increase trust and closeness towards one's own group while increasing defensiveness towards outsiders. Some well-known early findings, like oxytocin nasal sprays increasing trust, haven't reliably replicated, a reminder to be cautious about headline-friendly science."],
    ["Cortisol and adrenaline", "Stress hormones prepare the body for action, but chronic stress causes harm.",
      "In a threat, adrenaline (also called epinephrine) rapidly raises heart rate and breathing, sends blood to muscles and sharpens alertness, the fight-or-flight response. Cortisol, released more slowly by the adrenal glands, raises blood sugar and helps the body cope with stress over hours. Cortisol also follows a daily rhythm, normally peaking soon after waking. These responses are life-saving in short bursts. But chronic stress, with cortisol elevated for weeks or months, is linked to poor sleep, weakened immunity, weight gain and higher risk of heart disease and depression."],
    ["Endorphins", "Endorphins are the body's natural painkillers, released during exercise, laughter and pain.",
      "Endorphins are the body's own opioids, reducing pain and producing feelings of wellbeing. They're released during injury, intense or prolonged exercise, and laughter, and they help explain why people sometimes feel little pain right after a serious injury. The 'runner's high' was long credited to endorphins, though research suggests endocannabinoids, the body's cannabis-like chemicals, also play a big role. Opioid drugs like morphine and oxycodone act on the same receptors, which is why they relieve pain so powerfully, and also why they can be addictive and dangerous in overdose."],
    ["Melatonin and the body clock", "Melatonin rises in darkness and signals to the body that it's night.",
      "Melatonin is a hormone made by the pineal gland, a small structure deep in the brain. Its release is controlled by a master clock in the hypothalamus that responds to light entering the eyes. Levels rise in the evening as it gets dark, stay high overnight and fall in the morning. Bright light at night, especially blue-rich light from screens and LEDs, can suppress it and delay sleepiness. Melatonin is less a knockout sleeping pill than a timing signal, which is why it's mainly used for jet lag and certain sleep timing problems. In Australia, melatonin is available only from a pharmacist or on prescription."],
    ["Beyond the myths", "Brain chemicals work as systems, so be sceptical of quick fixes that promise to 'hack' them.",
      "Popular advice often promises to boost or reset one chemical, from 'dopamine detox' to foods that raise serotonin. Brain chemistry doesn't work that simply, and there's little evidence for most such claims. The principles behind them can still be sensible: cutting back on constant notifications can make everyday activities feel more rewarding, and regular exercise, daylight, good sleep and time with people you care about all support mood and stress resilience. If low mood, anxiety or stress persists for weeks, talk to a GP, who can discuss options including therapy and medication."],
  ],
  [
    [1, "What does research suggest dopamine is mostly about?", ["Pleasure itself", "Sleep", "Wanting, motivation and learning from rewards", "Digestion"], 2, "Unpredictable rewards tap into it."],
    [2, "Where is most of the body's serotonin found?", ["The gut", "The brain", "The heart", "The blood"], 0, "Around 90%."],
    [2, "What did a 2022 review find about serotonin and depression?", ["Low serotonin clearly causes depression", "No consistent evidence that low serotonin is the simple cause", "Serotonin cures depression", "Serotonin doesn't exist"], 1, "Antidepressants still help many people."],
    [4, "When do cortisol levels normally peak each day?", ["Soon after waking", "Just before sleep", "In the middle of the night", "After lunch"], 0, "Cortisol follows a daily rhythm, normally peaking soon after you wake."],
    [5, "What are endorphins?", ["Stress hormones", "The body's natural painkillers", "Digestive enzymes", "Vitamins"], 1, "Released during exercise and laughter."],
  ]);

WB.L("dreams", "body", "beginner", "Why we dream",
  "You spend about six years of your life dreaming. Scientists still debate why, but they've learned a lot about how.",
  [
    ["Catching REM", "REM sleep was discovered in the early 1950s, when a student noticed sleepers' eyes darting beneath their lids.",
      "In the early 1950s Eugene Aserinsky, a graduate student in Nathaniel Kleitman's sleep lab at the University of Chicago, wired sleepers to an old brainwave machine, starting with his eight-year-old son, Armond. Several times a night the pens traced bursts of rapid, jerky eye movements, while the brain's activity looked almost awake. At first he suspected the machine was faulty. In 1953 he and Kleitman published their discovery of rapid eye movement, or REM, sleep. A few years later William Dement, in the same lab, woke volunteers at different moments. Roused from REM, most described vivid dreams; roused at other times, far fewer did. Dreams do happen outside REM, but they tend to be shorter and less story-like."],
    ["Why we forget them", "Most dreams are forgotten within minutes of waking.",
      "Most people dream several times a night but remember few dreams, and some people rarely recall any. Brain chemistry during sleep, including low levels of noradrenaline, seems to make it hard to store dreams in long-term memory. If you wake during or just after a dream, you're more likely to remember it, which is why an alarm during REM can leave you with a vivid fragment. Keeping a notebook by the bed and writing dreams down immediately on waking improves recall, as does simply intending to remember. Lying still with your eyes closed for a moment before moving can help too."],
    ["What we dream about", "Dreams mostly feature familiar people, places and worries, often with strong emotions.",
      "Researchers have collected tens of thousands of dream reports. Psychologist Calvin Hall began systematically cataloguing them in the mid-20th century, and his coding system is still used. Most dreams are less bizarre than we assume: they often feature familiar people and settings, and the recent concerns of waking life. Negative emotions such as fear, anxiety and frustration are common. Some themes appear across many cultures, including being chased, falling, flying, and being late or unprepared for an exam. Dreams rarely involve reading, writing or using a phone, perhaps because the brain regions for those tasks are less active during REM."],
    ["Theories of dreaming", "Theories range from Freud's wish fulfilment to memory processing and threat simulation.",
      "Sigmund Freud, in his book The Interpretation of Dreams, published in late 1899 though dated 1900, argued dreams disguise hidden wishes, a theory few scientists accept today. In 1977, the activation-synthesis hypothesis proposed dreams are the brain's attempt to make sense of random activity from the brainstem. Other theories suggest dreams help process emotions, consolidate memories, or rehearse responses to threats, an idea put forward by Finnish researcher Antti Revonsuo in 2000. Many researchers think dreaming may serve several functions, or be partly a by-product of what sleep does for the brain."],
    ["Dreams and emotion", "REM sleep may help process emotional experiences, taking the sting out of them.",
      "Sleep researcher Matthew Walker and others have suggested REM sleep acts as 'overnight therapy', helping the brain process emotional memories while stress chemicals like noradrenaline are low. Some studies suggest people react less intensely to emotional images after a night's sleep, though findings are mixed. In post-traumatic stress disorder, nightmares can replay trauma, and this process may break down. Therapies such as imagery rehearsal, rewriting a nightmare's ending while awake and rehearsing the new version, can reduce nightmares. Poor sleep also makes people more emotionally reactive the next day."],
    ["Lucid dreaming", "In lucid dreams, people know they're dreaming, and sometimes control the dream.",
      "In a lucid dream, you become aware that you're dreaming while still asleep. In the 1970s and 80s, researchers including Keith Hearne and Stephen LaBerge showed lucid dreaming was real: sleepers in labs signalled with pre-agreed eye movements while brain recordings confirmed they were in REM sleep. Around half of people report having had at least one lucid dream, though frequent lucid dreaming is much rarer. Techniques to increase them include reality checks during the day, like asking 'am I dreaming?', and setting a clear intention to recognise dreams before falling asleep."],
    ["Do animals dream?", "Many animals have REM-like sleep, and rats appear to replay their day while sleeping.",
      "REM-like sleep has been found in mammals and birds, and signs of similar sleep states have been reported in some reptiles and even octopuses. We can't ask animals what they experience, but there are clues. In a well-known 2001 study, researchers recorded neurons in rats' hippocampus as they ran a maze, then found similar firing patterns replayed during the rats' REM sleep. Pet owners often see dogs twitch, paddle their legs or whimper in sleep. Whether animals experience anything like human dreams is unknown, but the replay findings support the idea that sleep helps process memories."],
    ["Nightmares and sleep problems", "Occasional nightmares are normal; frequent ones can be treated.",
      "Nightmares are common, especially in children and during stressful times. Frequent, distressing nightmares may be linked to anxiety, trauma, some medications, alcohol or sleep disorders. Sleepwalking and sleep talking usually happen in non-REM sleep, not during dreams. REM sleep behaviour disorder, where people act out dreams because muscle paralysis fails, is uncommon but important to diagnose, as it can be an early sign of Parkinson's disease and related conditions. Regular sleep hours and a calm wind-down routine help sleep overall. If nightmares or sleep problems are affecting your life, a GP can help or refer you to a sleep specialist."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "Who first described REM sleep, in 1953?", ["Sigmund Freud and Carl Jung", "Eugene Aserinsky and Nathaniel Kleitman", "Calvin Hall", "Stephen LaBerge"], 1, "Their discovery opened up the modern science of sleep and dreaming."],
    [1, "How can you remember dreams better?", ["Write them down immediately on waking", "Sleep longer", "Eat cheese", "Avoid sleep"], 0, "Intention helps too."],
    [3, "What did Freud argue dreams were?", ["Random noise", "Disguised wish fulfilment", "Memory storage", "Messages from gods"], 1, "Few scientists accept this today."],
    [4, "What might REM sleep do for emotional memories?", ["Make them worse", "Delete them", "Nothing", "Help process them, taking the sting out"], 3, "'Overnight therapy'."],
    [5, "How did researchers show lucid dreaming was real?", ["Brain surgery", "Sleepers signalled with pre-agreed eye movements", "Interviews", "Hypnosis"], 1, "In the 1970s and 80s."],
  ]);

WB.L("consciousness", "body", "advanced", "The mystery of consciousness",
  "Why does it feel like something to be you? Science can map the brain in astonishing detail, yet this question remains unsolved.",
  [
    ["What we're trying to explain", "Consciousness is subjective experience: what it feels like to be you.",
      "Right now, you're experiencing colours, sounds, thoughts and feelings. Philosopher Thomas Nagel captured this in a famous 1974 essay, 'What Is It Like to Be a Bat?': a creature is conscious if there is something it is like to be that creature. A bat navigating by echolocation presumably has experiences, but ones we may never be able to imagine. Scientists can study which brain processes accompany experiences, but explaining why there's any experience at all, rather than just information processing in the dark, is extraordinarily hard."],
    ["The hard problem", "Philosopher David Chalmers called explaining subjective experience 'the hard problem'.",
      "In the mid-1990s, Australian philosopher David Chalmers distinguished 'easy problems', explaining how the brain processes information, directs attention or controls behaviour, from 'the hard problem': why any of this is accompanied by subjective experience. The easy problems aren't actually easy, but they're the kind science knows how to tackle. The hard problem seems different in kind. Chalmers illustrated it with the idea of a philosophical zombie, a being physically identical to you but with no inner experience. Some philosophers think the problem will dissolve with more knowledge; others think it reveals a deep gap in our understanding."],
    ["Searching for the correlates", "Scientists look for brain activity that reliably matches conscious experience.",
      "Many neuroscientists study the neural correlates of consciousness: the minimal brain activity needed for a specific experience. In experiments like binocular rivalry, each eye sees a different image, but people consciously see only one at a time, switching back and forth. Researchers compare brain activity when the same image is seen or not seen. Such studies show much brain processing happens unconsciously, and only some reaches awareness. In blindsight, people with damage to the visual cortex can point to objects they insist they can't see, showing vision without conscious experience."],
    ["Competing theories", "Leading theories include Global Workspace Theory and Integrated Information Theory.",
      "Global Workspace Theory, developed by Bernard Baars and extended by Stanislas Dehaene, proposes that information becomes conscious when it's broadcast widely across the brain, like being put on a stage for all systems to use. Integrated Information Theory, from Giulio Tononi, proposes consciousness corresponds to how much a system integrates information, measured as a quantity called phi. In 2023, a large 'adversarial collaboration' tested predictions of both, with mixed results. The debate grew heated, with some researchers publicly questioning whether Integrated Information Theory counts as science at all."],
    ["Radical alternatives", "Some thinkers argue experience is an illusion; others think it's everywhere.",
      "Not everyone accepts the hard problem as usually framed. Illusionists, influenced by philosopher Daniel Dennett, argue that the sense of an inner, private experience is itself a kind of trick the brain plays, and that explaining why we believe we have it is the real task. At the other extreme, panpsychists, such as philosopher Philip Goff, suggest consciousness may be a basic feature of matter, present in simple forms everywhere. Most scientists sit somewhere in between, assuming consciousness arises from brain activity but unsure how. None of these views has decisive evidence, and each has serious critics."],
    ["Altered states", "Sleep, anaesthesia, meditation and psychedelics show consciousness can change dramatically.",
      "Consciousness isn't simply on or off. It fades in deep sleep, transforms in dreams, and vanishes under general anaesthesia, which doctors use safely every day but still don't fully understand. Patients in comas or vegetative states raise hard questions: in a landmark 2006 study, brain scans showed an apparently unresponsive patient following instructions, like imagining playing tennis, with the same brain activity as healthy volunteers. Later studies found similar hidden awareness in a minority of such patients. Meditation and psychedelic drugs also alter consciousness in ways researchers are now studying for clues."],
    ["Animals and machines", "Which animals, and whether future AI, might be conscious, are open questions.",
      "In 2012, a group of scientists signed the Cambridge Declaration on Consciousness, stating that many animals, including mammals, birds and octopuses, possess the brain structures needed for consciousness. A 2024 New York declaration went further, suggesting there's at least a realistic possibility of consciousness in reptiles, fish and some invertebrates. The question grows more urgent with artificial intelligence. Current AI systems can produce convincingly human-like language, but whether anything like experience accompanies that remains deeply uncertain, and without a theory of consciousness, it's hard to know how we'd ever tell."],
    ["Why it matters", "How we understand consciousness shapes medicine, animal welfare and how we treat AI.",
      "This isn't just an armchair puzzle. Doctors need to judge whether an unresponsive patient might be aware, which affects care and end-of-life decisions. Anaesthetists work to prevent the rare but distressing experience of awareness during surgery. Laws on animal welfare increasingly depend on which creatures can feel pain; the UK, for example, recognised octopuses, crabs and lobsters as sentient in a 2022 law. And as AI becomes more capable, societies will face questions about whether machines could ever deserve moral consideration. Staying curious, and humble about what we don't know, is the honest position."],
  ],
  [
    [0, "What was Thomas Nagel's famous essay called?", ["I Think Therefore I Am", "The Hard Problem", "The Conscious Mind", "What Is It Like to Be a Bat?"], 3, "Published in 1974."],
    [1, "What is 'the hard problem' of consciousness?", ["Building faster computers", "Explaining why brain processing is accompanied by subjective experience", "Curing dementia", "Measuring IQ"], 1, "Named by David Chalmers in the mid-1990s."],
    [2, "What does binocular rivalry show?", ["Consciousness switches between competing images", "We see two images at once", "Eyes don't matter", "Colour blindness"], 0, "Much processing is unconscious."],
    [3, "What does Global Workspace Theory propose?", ["Consciousness is in the heart", "Only humans are conscious", "Information becomes conscious when broadcast widely across the brain", "Consciousness is an illusion"], 2, "Like being put on a stage."],
    [6, "What did the Cambridge Declaration on Consciousness state?", ["Only humans are conscious", "Many animals possess the brain structures for consciousness", "AI is conscious", "Consciousness doesn't exist"], 1, "Signed in 2012."],
  ]);
